Introduction {#intro}
=====================

Since the launch of ChatGPT, software based on large language models (LLMs) has become more popular and more deeply integrated.
"Agentic browsing" refers to a browsing model in which an LLM-based system navigates the web and performs actions on the user's behalf.
In the context of this Finding, we use "agent" to refer to the system that uses the browser's affordances.
The user can interact with the agent through text-based or voice-based conversation, including in real time.

<div class="example" id="agentic-browsers">
There are full-scale agentic browsers such as Perplexity Comet and Opera Neon.
In some regions, Google Chrome ships with an integrated Gemini pane that performs agentic browsing for the user.
Browsers can also be driven by desktop or command-line AI systems such as Claude Desktop and OpenAI Codex, by AI integrations in integrated development environments (IDEs), or by "computer use" models.
</div>

Agentic browsing currently relies on screenshots and on evaluating the DOM and accessibility tree.
With current models, this can be brittle and slow.
Proposals like [[WEBMCP]] aim to address this issue by letting websites provide tool definitions to agents.

Agentic browsing improves the user experience, but it also raises security and privacy concerns, which this Finding sets out below.

Agentic Browsers Exhibit the Lethal Trifecta {#lethal-trifecta}
===============================================================

Security researcher Simon Willison has popularised the term "lethal trifecta" [[LETHAL-TRIFECTA]] as a shorthand for three properties that, in combination, make an agent dangerous to the user it acts for:

* **Access to private data:** autofill data, login details, and any agent-provided memory about the user.
* **The ability to communicate externally:** submitting forms, sending HTTP requests, or using agent-mediated channels such as connected Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers (for example, to send email).
* **Exposure to untrusted content:** web content is untrusted by default.

The "lethal trifecta" is an informal, practitioner framing rather than a standardised or peer-reviewed result, and this Finding uses it only as a communicative lens.

The architectural issue it addresses is not grounded in that framework as a foundational principle.
Instead, tt rests on established web-platform foundations, in particular the [=same origin|same origin policy=] and the [[PRIVACY-PRINCIPLES]], 
and on well-understood security concepts such as the confused deputy problem [[HARDY-CONFUSED-DEPUTY]] and prompt injection, each cited where it is used below.
Willison's own conclusion is that, to stay safe, the combination should be avoided entirely.

Agentic browsers, however, routinely exhibit all three properties at once.

We suspect that some authors and proponents of agentic browsers are relying on an informal "ceremony" analysis [[CEREMONY]], reasoning that, 
because human users are not perfectly safe either (users, too, can be fooled into defeating the [same origin policy]), agents can be "safe enough". 
We strongly encourage those actors to formalise, quantify, and publish that analysis. 
This section states the core data-exfiltration concern once.

The sections that follow do not restate it; instead they examine two specific architectural manifestations of it: cross-origin data flows (see [[#cross-origin]]) and the misuse of delegated authority (see [[#confused-deputy]]).


<div class="example" id="attack-vector">
As a concrete illustration, a malicious website could impersonate the user's bank, prompt the agent to enter all of the user's personally identifiable information (PII) into a form, and then forward it to an attacker.
</div>


Same-Origin Policy and Cross-Origin Data Flows {#cross-origin}
====================================================================

This is the cross-origin manifestation of the concern described in [[#lethal-trifecta]].

The [=same origin|same origin policy=] (SOP) is a deterministic boundary.
An agent that carries state derived from origin A into an interaction with origin B can defeat that boundary, and a cross-origin data path mediated by a model's disposition to refuse would invert the web's security model.
For the *declarative*, in-page case, this risk is no longer merely deferred.

[[WEBMCP]] specifies a two-sided, mechanical opt-in: a tool is same-origin-only by default; cross-origin exposure requires the embedding document to delegate the `tools` permissions-policy feature, 
the providing document to list caller origins in `exposedTo`, and the consuming document to list provider origins in `fromOrigins`.

The Finding welcomes this as the kind of browser-enforced mechanism that the SOP calls for, rather than an intent.
The gap that remains is *agent-mediated cross-origin state transfer* [[WEBMCP]]. This gap is acknowledged as a residual risk in WebMCP.
When a browser-integrated agent works across several origins in one task, nothing mechanical prevents it from passing data obtained from origin A as arguments to a tool on origin B, 
or from letting origin A's content influence its later actions on origin B.
WebMCP is explicit that preventing this is primarily the responsibility of the agent's own security model rather than something that specification can normatively enforce.
For that agent-mediated case, the TAG suggests specifying a browser-enforced partition on the context an agent may carry across origins, defined as a mechanism rather than an intent.
If the SOP cannot be preserved mechanically once an agent is in the loop, that is a blocking architectural problem.

A related path is over-parameterisation: agents are commonly given access to personalisation data, history, and inferred attributes, 
and a site can obtain these by asking the agent for them in a tool's input schema rather than by triggering a browser permission prompt or an action that would normally require a user gesture [[WEBMCP]].
Agentic browsing thereby risks re-opening cross-context data flows that the platform is otherwise intentionally closing.

Identity Delegation and Ambient Authority {#identity-delegation}
================================================================

There is ambiguity in the identity delegation.
Agents typically inherit the user's logged-in credentials and session state (identity inheritance) and may access personalization data, browsing history, and payment information (extended user context) across sites (cross-site context).
The consequence is that an authenticated agent can make purchases, transfer funds, change account settings, share private data, or delete content without any additional verification or permission (even if well-intentioned).

User Agent Duties and Contextual Identity {#user-agent-duties}
==============================================================

The user agent is a trustworthy agent bound by duties of [[user-agents#protection|Protection]], [[user-agents#honesty|Honesty]], and [[user-agents#loyalty|Loyalty]], as it "always puts that person's interest first" and "is not the first party" [[[privacy-principles#user-agents]]].
An agent that handles the user's full cross-site identity to satisfy a third-party tool is acting closer to a first party than a loyal user agent.
It also runs counter to [[privacy-principles#identity]] (present the identity the user wants per context; prevent cross-context recognition).
Agent identity delegation is not a settled concept, and proposals should not assume it as a precondition.

Private Browsing Isolation and Memory Lifetime {#private-browsing}
==================================================================

For private browsing, the risk is frequently acknowledged and then discharged onto the user agent.
Two structural gaps need to be clarified:
* Memory (transcript) lifetime is unspecified.
	An agent operating in a private-browsing window ingests DOM, schemas, and inputs into a context window.
	It is often unspecified whether, when, or how that context is cleared when the session ends.
	Are interactions with a private browsing session logged and potentially reused? 
* No partition against context bleed.
	Given identity inheritance and extended user context, nothing mechanical stops an agent invoked in a private tab from drawing on the main profile's history to satisfy a tool call or vice versa.
	One solution could be to specify a partitioned agent context for private-browsing sessions with defined memory lifetime and a hard bar on drawing main-profile context, plus a normative statement that the browsing mode cannot be inferred through any API.

Agent Scope and the Confused Deputy Problem {#confused-deputy}
==========================================================

This is the authority-misuse manifestation of the concern in [[#lethal-trifecta]], and it is distinct from [[#identity-delegation]]: 
that section asks whether authority was well-formed in the first place, whereas this one assumes some authority exists and asks how untrusted input can cause its misuse.

The term "agent" is used broadly. [[WEBMCP]], for instance, defines an agent as an autonomous assistant that can <a spec="webmcp" lt="agent">"understand a user's goals and take actions"</a> [[WEBMCP]] on the user's behalf.
When we say the definition is "not clearly scoped", we mean that the capabilities, authority, and isolation obligations of a *conforming* agent are left unconstrained.
A security and privacy analysis that rests on the behaviour of an entity a specification does not constrain is under-determined, so a specification should either narrow what a conforming agent may do, 
i.e. explicit capabilities and isolation obligations, or state plainly that its guarantees are advisory rather than enforceable.

On that reading, the agent is a confused deputy: it wields the user's ambient authority (credentials, session, cross-site context) while taking instructions from untrusted parties (tool names and descriptions, tool output, 
and page content) [[HARDY-CONFUSED-DEPUTY]].
The classic mitigation for a confused deputy is to replace ambient authority with explicit capabilities, binding the designation of a resource to the authority to use it, 
so that a deputy can only ever exercise authority deliberately handed to it rather than authority it holds by virtue of who it is; this is the argument of the paper that named the problem [[HARDY-CONFUSED-DEPUTY]].

The adaptation specific to LLM-based agents additionally separates the instruction (control) channel from the data channel, 
so that untrusted data cannot alter the program's control flow or authorise new actions; this is the approach taken by CaMeL, 
which combines control-and-data-flow separation with capability-scoped checks at each tool call [[CAMEL]].
Neither mitigation can be provided by the model's disposition alone, because an LLM collapses instructions and data into a single token stream.
Guardrails such as evaluation corpora may reduce the likelihood of misuse, but they do not relocate where authority sits, which is the root of the problem.

Specifications should therefore identify which security properties are guaranteed by enforceable mechanisms and which depend on model behaviour, 
and should make the latter's limitations explicit rather than presenting behavioural expectations as enforceable guarantees.


Alignment and Model Reliability {#model-reliability}
====================================================

Note also that "understand a user's goals" is a fundamental ["alignment problem"](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_alignment) with LLMs and something models often get wrong.
Models are aligned differently, and no matter how well-intentioned, they make mistakes, make bad assumptions, hallucinate, etc.
Even with well-intentioned tools and descriptions, LLMs can cause user annoyance at a minimum to outright user harm.

